{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788304563,"nonce":"7KOv7/3A+4+oLAEehbYg05KNxJk=","title":"Weapons: no vanilla alert covers armament and ResourceCounter cannot count one, so the harness still cannot say how armed the colony is","message":"Split out of 2d9a1da by the rates worker, session 22, rather than smuggled into\nit. 2d9a1da shipped `history` (the game's eleven recorders) and `trends` (a\n2,500-tick sampler over what the game does not record). Weapons is the one\nindicator Evan named that NEITHER half could deliver, and the reason is\nstructural rather than a scoping choice.\n\nTHE GAP, from that issue's own research and re-checked here\n\nEvan named food, medicine and **especially weapons** (\"since raids will knock\nyou out\"), and called weapons the sharpest of the three. The research on\n2d9a1da confirmed it at source level:\n\n- Across all 133 vanilla alert classes, **NONE covers armament.** The three\n  weapon-related alerts are per-pawn mismatches; `Alert_NeedDefenses` is\n  buildings-only and fires only on days 2-5.\n- `ResourceCounter` cannot help — weapons are Uncounted, so\n  `digest.resources.*` will never see one however the counter is queried.\n\nSo a colony can field six colonists and two guns and the game never says a word,\nand after 2d9a1da the harness still cannot either.\n\nWHY 2d9a1da COULD NOT CLOSE IT\n\n`ColonySampler` is deliberately a PERSISTENCE LAYER OVER AN EXISTING OBSERVER:\nit calls `DigestVerb.SectionFor(map, …)` and lifts named scalars out of\ndictionaries the digest already builds. That is what makes it cheap (every\naccessor it touches is already audited) and what makes it survive changes to\n`food_days` made on other branches. **Nothing in the digest publishes a weapons\ncount, so there is nothing for the sampler to sample.** A weapons figure is a\nNEW DERIVATION and belongs in the observation surface first; sampling it is then\none row in `ColonySampler.Fields`.\n\nWHAT THE DERIVATION HAS TO BE, and why it is not one query\n\nEquipped and spare are DISJOINT POPULATIONS and both have to be walked:\n\n- EQUIPPED: a pawn scan. `Pawn_EquipmentTracker.Primary` per colonist. A pawn\n  holding a weapon is not in any lister group a haul query would reach.\n- SPARE: `map.listerThings.ThingsInGroup(ThingRequestGroup.Weapon)`, which is\n  the ground/stockpile half and does NOT include anything a pawn is carrying.\n\nAdding them is the count; publishing only one of them is a wrong number that\nlooks right. Both routes need checking against `WorldSafe`/`PawnSafe`\ndiscipline before they go anywhere near the digest, and `ThingsInGroup` is\nalready used by `DigestVerb.ThreatSection` for `Fire`, so there is a shape to\ncopy.\n\nOpen questions the implementer resolves by investigation, not by asking:\n\n1. Does \"weapon\" mean `IsWeapon`, `ThingRequestGroup.Weapon`, or\n   `equipmentType == Primary`? They differ over grenades, shields, and\n   `Weapon_MeleeWood` style tools. Cite the member.\n2. Is a wooden club a weapon for this purpose? A count that says 6/6 armed on\n   six clubs against a mech cluster is worse than no count. Some quality or\n   damage axis is probably needed, and `GetStatValueAbstract` is on session 19's\n   forbidden list for a predicate — so if the answer needs a stat, say what that\n   costs and where it may be read.\n3. Forbidden and unreachable spares: `54b0c9a` established that a count with no\n   reachability test manufactures a verdict (`short_by: 185` with 869 logs ten\n   cells away). `Materials.Of` is the precedent for asking the colonists' own\n   question.\n4. Does this belong in `digest.colonists` (per pawn), a new `defence` section\n   (colony scalar), or both? A per-pawn `armed` flag is what `triage`-shaped\n   verbs want; `weapons_per_colonist` is what a trend wants.\n\nACCEPTANCE\n\n- The digest publishes a weapons reading whose EQUIPPED and SPARE halves are\n  separable and whose total is their sum, with the def-set rule stated by member\n  name and the \"is a club a weapon\" question resolved by investigation and\n  recorded in DESIGN's decisions log.\n- Cheap on session 19's axis (no `Room.Role`, no pathfind) or, if it is not,\n  measured and justified with the measurement — it is a candidate predicate\n  section and a candidate sample field, both of which run on a schedule.\n- Acceptance proves the two populations are disjoint and both counted: a\n  colonist holding the only rifle on the map must produce equipped:1 spare:0\n  total:1, and dropping it must produce equipped:0 spare:1 total:1 with no\n  change to the total.\n- No accessor adopted mutates on read, demonstrated the way 2.4 did it.\n- ONE ROW added to `ColonySampler.Fields` so `trends` carries the slope, and the\n  durable sample file's header row grows by that column. Additive only: the\n  column set is append-never-rename by contract (2d9a1da), and adding it late\n  is exactly the case that contract exists to make safe.\n- The M1 counterfactual answered on real data: with a weapons series, would the\n  M1 post-mortem's \"we died to a raid\" have become \"we died to a raid at 14\n  food-days and 0.3 weapons per colonist\"? That sentence is 4.1's whole reason\n  for wanting this issue.","files":null}]}